### 1.2 What is refused and where

`modules/autodiff/pullback.cpp`, `Backward::run` (activity loop, around line 2424):

- Activity starts at the **ports whose gradient this pass computes**, not at every port that
  has a tangent. `Backward` now takes that set (`differentiated_`): for `grad`/`with_grad` it is
  the first `with` parameter only; for a function pullback it is every port for which the plan
  returns a tangent. An ordinary argument (`x`, `labels`, a scale) is data even when its type
  has a tangent type (every `Tensor` does): no gradient is being computed for it, so nothing
  derived from it can lose one.
- A value whose type has no tangent (`tangentTypeOf(type) == nullptr`) and that depends on such a
  differentiated port becomes **tainted**. “Depends” follows Norm and With inputs, not Ctrl
  inputs: control edges still select code and are not data.
- A node that **has** a tangent but receives a tainted value as Norm/With data is refused with
  `failAt(nodeOrigin(node), ...)`. The message names the value type, the location of the use, and
  (when known) where the no-channel value came from:
  `a value of type '{}' depends on a differentiated parameter but has no gradient channel, and it is used as data here{}...`
- Aggregates that are built from tainted parts are not refused as a whole: a tuple/struct FILL
  with a tangent is structural, so a struct can carry a tainted field and a later read of that
  field is the located use. This keeps `if`, tuples and struct construction working.
- Three kinds of inputs are deliberately control, not data:
  - a branch condition (`BRCH`'s `bool`);
  - a `JOIN`'s arm selector (`BRCH`);
  - a `tensor:where` / `:op/where` mask (its first Norm input).
  A tainted value used under one of these still refuses when it is later converted to a number
  (e.g. `float(bool_value)`).
- Shape/length metadata (`tensor:shape`, `tensor:rank`, `tensor:numel`, `:op/len_arr`,
  `:op/len_str`) is not tainted: a shape is a discrete property, and reshaping by it is control.
  `tensor:numel` is the count in `sum(x) / (numel(x) as float)`, so the definition graph of
  `tensor_definitions::mean` differentiates.
- `visitOper`'s missing-derivative-rule error is now located too
  (`no derivative rule for operator '{}' of type '{}'...`), instead of an unlocated `fail`.

**The false positive this fixes.** C2's `with_grad(loss)` has one differentiated `with`
parameter (`m`, the model tree); `x` and `labels` are ordinary arguments. Before the fix all
graph ports with a tangent were seeds, and every `Tensor` has one, so `labels` was “active”.
`cast(labels, 'int64')` therefore tainted the integer tensor, and `softmax_cross_entropy`, which
also reads the active logits, refused at `train.cml:53:12`. The labels are data: nothing upstream
of them is differentiated and no gradient is being dropped. The same root caused a float input
converted with `int(...)` and then back to float to be refused. The fix is the seed set, not a
loosening of the use check: a value is tainted only when it is downstream of a port whose
gradient is actually being computed.

`modules/autodiff/pullback.cpp`, `ForwardRewrite::rewriteLoop` (around line 1527):

- The body plan records `stateNextTainted[j]`: whether the next value of state `j` is tainted.
  If the state has no gradient channel and its next value depends on an active/tainted value, the
  loop is refused at the loop's own origin:
  `a 'loop' state of type '{}' has no gradient channel, but its next value depends on a differentiated parameter{}...`
- This catches an array state that the result does not read, and a loop whose value has no
  tangent but whose initial state depends on the parameter. The previous check only looked at
  `active_`, so an initial array/boolean derived from the parameter could slip through; it now
  checks `tainted_` as well.

`src/builtin/operators/derivative.cpp`:

- `:struct/copy_arr` now has the identity rule (`identityVjp`). The in-place loop rewrite copies
  a literal initial array through this private operator; without a rule, a multi-state loop with
  a `float[]` state failed with “no derivative rule”. It is a copy, so its gradient is the
  output gradient unchanged.
- `:struct/append_arr` and `:struct/append_arr_owned` already had the append rule.

